# Comments on generated code

When writing or changing code, add comments that explain **why**, constraints, or non-obvious behavior. Match comment length to the complexity of the nearby code. Do not comment every line or narrate what the syntax already shows.

## Do

- Comment routing decisions, fallbacks, timeouts, retries, and error-status choices.
- Comment business rules (issue types, labels, assignee matching, trigger names).
- Comment injection, mapping lookup order, and test helpers that exist for a specific reason.
- Keep comments to one or two short sentences unless the block truly needs more.

## Do not

- Restate obvious assignments, returns, or standard Express/HTTP boilerplate.
- Write long paragraphs above simple getters, constants whose names are already clear, or one-line wrappers.
- Leave secrets, tokens, or full payloads in comments.

## Examples

```js
// ❌ BAD — restates the code
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000; // set the port

// ✅ GOOD — skip; the names are enough
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
```

```js
// ❌ BAD — too long for a simple filter
// Iterate every changelog item because Jira sometimes sends many fields...

// ✅ GOOD — why the scan exists
// Assignee may not be changelog.items[0]; search the full list.
const assignedToCursor = changelogItems(body).some(
	(item) => item.fieldId === 'assignee' && item.toString === CURSOR_DISPLAY_NAME
);
```

```js
// ✅ GOOD — non-obvious HTTP choice
// 202: Jira delivered a valid event we intentionally do not forward.
return res.status(202).json({ forwarded: false, reason });
```
